<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.7.3: Different Methods of Checking Solutions of Quadratic Equations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02447</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.7.3: Different Methods of Checking Solutions of Quadratic Equations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>3318d1c5-c5c8-47c8-8f62-d3fe41f934a8</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>The equation \(h(t)=2+30t−5t^2\) represents the height, as a function of time, of a pumpkin that was catapulted up in the air. Height is measured in meters and time is measured in seconds.</item>
</list>
<section class="check-understanding"><title>Activity</title><exercise id="6982616e-89d3-4477-83e6-257950690eaa" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter the number of seconds after launch.</para><para>The pumpkin reached a maximum height of 47 meters. How many seconds after launch did that happen?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The pumpkin reached a maximum height of 47 meters 3 seconds after launch.</para><para>The correct answer is 3. The pumpkin reached a maximum height of 47 meters 3 seconds after launch.</para></solution></exercise></section>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Show your reasoning.</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your Answer:</para>
  
  <para>\(\begin{array}{l}
    h(t)=-5 t^{2}+30 t+2 \\
    -5 t^{2}+30 t+2=47 \\
    -5 t^{2}+30 t-45=0 \\
    a=-5, b=30, c=-45 \\
    t=\frac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^{2}-4 a c}}{2 a} \\
    t=\frac{-(30) \pm \sqrt{(30)^{2}-(4)(-5)(-45)}}{2(-5)} \\
    t=\frac{-30 \pm \sqrt{900-900}}{-10} \\
    t=\frac{-30}{-10} \\
    t=3
    \end{array}\)</para>

</solution></exercise>
</example>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Suppose someone was unconvinced by your solution. Find another way (besides the steps you already took) to show your solution is correct.</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your Answer:</para>
  
  <list>
    <item> Substitute 3 for \(t\) in the original expression; evaluating it gives \(2+30(3)−5(3)^2\) or \(2+90−45\), which is 47. </item>
    <item> Graphing \(y=2+30t−5t^2\) and \(y=47\) shows an intersection is located at \((3,47)\). </item>
    <item> Graphing \(y=-45+30t−5t^2\) shows a zero at \((3,0)\). </item>
  </list>

</solution></exercise>
</example>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>The equation \(r(p)=80p−p^2\) models the revenue a band expects to collect as a function of the price of one concert ticket. Ticket prices and revenues are in dollars.</para>
    <para>A band member says that a ticket price of either $15.50 or $74.50 would generate approximately $1000 in revenue. Do you agree? Show your reasoning.</para>
  
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your Answer:</para>
  
  <list>
    <item> Partially agree. A ticket price of $15.50 will generate about $1000 in revenue, but a ticket price of $74.50 will generate much less (about $410). </item>
    <item> Using the <term data-schema-version="“1.0”" data-store="glossary-tooltip">quadratic formula</term> to solve \(1000=80p−p^2\) gives \(p \approx 15.50\) and \(p \approx 64.50\) as the solutions. </item>
    <item> Substituting 15.50 into the expression \(80p−p^2\) gives 999.75, which is close to 1000. \((80(15.50)−(15.50)2=999.75)\). Substituting 74.50 into the expression \(80p−p^2\) gives 409.75. </item>
    <item> Graphing the equation \(y=r(p)\) and \(y=1000\) shows two intersections at approximately \((15.50,1000)\) and \((64.50,1000)\). </item>
  </list>

</solution></exercise>
</example>

