</example><!--Start interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
<exercise data-wait-for-event="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
    
    <para>Function \(g\) is defined by the equation \(g(t)=2+30t−5t^2−47\). Its graph opens downward.</para>
    <para>Find the zeros of function \(g\) without graphing.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the zeros of function \(g\).</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Function \(g\) has one zero: 3.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>

<!--End interaction-->
<exercise data-wait-for-event="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <para>Show your reasoning.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your reasoning. </para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <list>
      <item> \(2+30t−5t^2−47\) is equivalent to \(-5t^2+30t−45\). Solving \(-5t^2+30t−45=0\) using the quadratic formula gives \(t=3\). </item>
      <item> \(-5t^2+30t−45\) can be rewritten as \(-5(t^2−6t+9)\) and then as \(-5(t−3)(t−3)\). Applying the zero product property to solve \(-5(t−3)(t−3)=0\) gives \(t=3\). </item>
    </list>
  
  </solution></exercise>

<!--End interaction-->
<exercise data-wait-for-event="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <para>Explain or show how the zeros you found can tell us the vertex of the graph of \(g\).</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your reasoning. </para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Function \(g\) only has one zero, which means its graph has only one horizontal intercept, occurring when \(t=3\). Because the point \((3,0)\) is the only intercept, it must also be the vertex of the graph, otherwise there would have been either two horizontal intercepts, or none.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>

<!--End interaction-->
<exercise data-wait-for-event="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <para>Recall that \(g(t)=2+30t−5t^2−47\) and \(h(t)=2+30t−5t^2\). Examine these functions \(g\) and \(h\) (which defined the height of the pumpkin). Explain how the maximum of function \(h\), once we know it, can tell us the maximum of \(g\).</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your reasoning. </para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>The value of the expression for \(g\) is 47 less than that of \(h\), so if we know the maximum of \(h\), we know that the maximum of \(g\) is 47 less than that. We saw that the maximum of \(h\) is 47, so the maximum of \(g\) is 47 less than 47, which is 0.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--End interaction--></section>
